{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And by these you shall become impure.</b> In Torat Kohanim they explained the verse's saying <b>and these</b> as referring to those that are yet to be mentioned below in the passage. And it is difficult for me: why did it say ״וּלְאֵלֶּה״ with the addition of the ו, seeing that this matter has no connection to what is above it, nor what is above with it?" "Or perhaps it intended to set the upper corresponding to the lower — meaning, every species of domesticated animal and wild beast, whether impure ones that neither chew the cud nor split the hoof, which were not enumerated here in the verse, or pure ones that chew the cud and split the hoof and are fully cloven, which were likewise not enumerated in the verse — and He alluded to them by saying ״לְכָל הַבְּהֵמָה״. And this general principle refers back to what He enumerated in the section on the signs of animals. Therefore it said ״וּלְאֵלֶּה״ with a ו, so that you not err by applying everything stated above; for this reason He specified that His words concern only domesticated animals and wild beasts." "Or it may intend to juxtapose the matter of impurity to the matter of eating stated in the preceding passage, etc.: just as eating requires a k'zayit, so too impurity requires a k'zayit." "And in Torat Kohanim they established that the verse comes only for the impurity of a limb from a living creature, whether of an impure animal or a pure animal, and they derived impurity of a k'zayit from another verse, as it is written (Leviticus 11:40): ״וְהָאֹכֵל מִנִּבְלָתָהּ יְכַבֵּס בְּגָדָיו וְטָמֵא עַד הָעָרֶב״ — and this is their language: it comes only to establish a measure for one who touches and one who carries — a k'zayit, like one who eats. And it is possible to say further that by this juxtaposition He alluded that if she gave birth to a species of animal adjacent to it, its law is the same." "And I have seen from Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra, of blessed memory, who wrote that in his opinion it appears that the word ״אֵלֶּה״ refers back to all those mentioned above, and he rejected the view of the one who says ״אֵלֶּה לְכָל הַבְּהֵמָה״ etc. And I say that it would have been better for him not to say something that is not heard, and not to disagree with those who receive the Torah — for who will listen to his words here, and even to learn them, when they are contrary to the received Torah of Moses? All the more so according to the plain sense of the verse, which even the youngest schoolchild would understand at first glance. And from whom shall I withhold what my eyes saw and more than that? And what power does he have to disagree with them even without necessity — and even if the opponent were his equal, all the more so those greater than him, and certainly and all the more so when the smallest of them is thicker than his waist! Go out and learn what the Amoraim said about the great ones who preceded them (Shabbat 112b): ״אִם הָרִאשׁוֹנִים״ etc. And to vindicate the Rabbi, of blessed memory, perhaps he did not write this interpretation except for a reason known to him, and he conducted himself after the manner of the Seventy Elders (Megillah 9a) who altered several matters. And for this too may HaShem make atonement for him."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {3 "The feminine singular verbs in the last clause (יָלְדָה, לָהּ) suggest the subject is a female animal giving birth; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:30.926991265Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}